{
  "thought_process": "Round 7 analysis: Current PID is P=13.5, I=0.0, D=0.0. Still in Phase 1 (P-only tuning). Historical progress: MAE: 4.37→3.15→2.19→1.63→1.16→0.87→0.64, RMS: 4.82→3.55→2.52→1.82→1.30→0.96→0.72. Consistent improvement continuing. The system is tracking a time-varying sinusoidal-like setpoint (r(t) oscillates between ~40 and ~60). Key observations this round: (1) MAE=0.64, RMS=0.72 - still improving but errors remain meaningful relative to the span of ~20. (2) Max absolute error=1.02, which is about 5% of the span - acceptable but room to improve. (3) Superimposed phase lag is evident: error peaks around +1.02 when setpoint is rising fast (around t=296010) and around -1.02 when setpoint is falling fast (around t=297990). This is a tracking lag characteristic - P alone cannot fully eliminate phase lag in tracking problems, but higher P will reduce the amplitude of tracking error. (4) Steady-state error estimate=0.50, which suggests there is still a persistent offset - this is expected with P-only control on a time-varying reference. (5) Zero crossings=2, which is consistent with the sinusoidal reference crossing zero-error twice per period - not indicative of oscillation. (6) Max overshoot peak=2.08% - very healthy, well within safe bounds. (7) PWM is saturating at ±15.8 near peak velocity of setpoint - this suggests the actuator is near its limits during fast setpoint changes. This is important: increasing P further may cause more aggressive PWM commands during transients, but since PWM is already near saturation during fast phases, the incremental benefit of higher P during those phases is limited. However, during slower phases (near setpoint reversals), higher P can still help reduce error. The system appears stable with no oscillation concerns. The per-round guardrail allows up to 3x current P = 40.5. Given the consistent improvement trend and the fact that PWM saturation is already occurring at peak velocity, I should continue increasing P but with some moderation. The improvement ratio from P=8→10.5 was MAE 1.16→0.87 (25% reduction), and from P=10.5→13.5 was MAE 0.87→0.64 (26% reduction). The trend is healthy. I'll try P=17.0, which is about 1.26x current value - a moderate increase consistent with the pattern. This keeps us well within the 3x guardrail and maintains conservative hardware-safe approach.",
  "analysis_summary": "Round 7 (P=13.5): MAE=0.64, RMS=0.72, max error=1.02, peak overshoot=2.08%. System tracks a ~20-span sinusoidal r(t) with consistent phase-lag type error (error peaks when setpoint changes fastest). PWM saturates near ±15.8 during peak velocity phases, indicating actuator is at limits during fast tracking. No oscillation detected (zero crossings=2 consistent with reference shape). Steady-state error=0.50 still present, expected with I=0. Overall trajectory: MAE 4.37→0.64 over 7 rounds, excellent monotonic improvement. System is stable and safe. Phase 1 (P-only) continues; P still has room to grow before diminishing returns or stability risk.",
  "tuning_action": "Increase P from 13.5 to 17.0 (factor ~1.26x, well within 3x guardrail). Keep I=0.0, D=0.0. Rationale: consistent ~25% MAE improvement per P increment, no oscillation signs, peak overshoot only 2.08%. PWM saturation during fast phases means further P increase has moderate benefit during peak velocity but still helps reduce error in slower phases. Conservative step to avoid pushing into oscillation territory. Will reassess after this round; if improvement slows significantly or overshoot increases, consider transitioning to Phase 2 (introduce small I to address residual tracking offset).",
